      "parameters": {
        "width": 480,
        "height": 1056,
        "content": "## Self-Healing Kubernetes Cluster - CrashLoopBackOff Auto-Remediation\n\n### How it works\n\nThis workflow detects Kubernetes pods stuck in CrashLoopBackOff from either an Alertmanager webhook or a scheduled poll of the Kubernetes API. It gathers pod status and logs, uses AI to diagnose the likely cause, then routes to an automated remediation path such as deleting the pod, scaling the deployment, rolling back, or escalating. After remediation or escalation, it builds an incident report, generates an AI-written summary, sends notifications, logs the incident, and responds to the webhook when applicable.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n- Configure the Kubernetes API server URL, namespace, and authentication credentials for all Kubernetes HTTP Request nodes.\n- Set up the Alertmanager webhook URL to point to the n8n Webhook trigger, and configure the schedule trigger interval if polling is required.\n- Add OpenAI credentials for both AI Agent nodes and their chat model subnodes.\n- Configure Slack incoming webhook credentials or URL for incident notifications.\n- Configure SendGrid API credentials and sender/recipient details for email reports.\n- Configure Google Sheets API credentials, spreadsheet ID, and target sheet/range for incident logging.\n- Review remediation permissions carefully; the Kubernetes service account needs read access to pods/logs/replicasets and write permissions for pod deletion, deployment scaling, and rollback patching.\n- Set workflow constants such as namespace, maximum replica count, and escalation defaults before enabling the workflow.\n"
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
